    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.planar_faces - EdgeCase\", \"isPartOf\":\n    {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.planar_faces.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.planar_faces()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: List the faces of a planar embedding of the graph.\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.planar_faces()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: List the faces of a planar embedding of the graph.\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.planar_faces()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n  .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: List the faces of a planar embedding of the graph.\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nList the faces of a planar embedding of the graph.\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L26289-L26400){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.planar_faces()\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Planar faces](../assets/graphs/planar-faces.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"A square A, B, C, D split by the diagonal A to C, drawn in the plane without crossings. Its embedding has three faces: the triangle A, B, C (tinted in the accent color), the triangle A, C, D (tinted teal), and the outer face, the region around the whole drawing, bounded by A, D, C, B (the dashed violet outline). The card lists each face as the cycle of nodes around it.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nA crossing-free drawing of a planar graph cuts the plane into regions, its *faces*,\none of them the unbounded outer face. Each face is described by the walk of nodes\naround its boundary. For a connected graph there are `e - n + 2` of them (Euler's\nformula), and every edge borders two faces or, for a bridge, the same face twice, so\nthe walks have `2e` steps in all. Faces are what planar duals, mesh processing, map\ncoloring and face-based planar algorithms work with.\n\nThe faces come from the embedding `check_planarity()` returns, traced as NetworkX's\n`PlanarEmbedding.faces()` traces them: start from a node and an edge leaving it, and at\neach node turn to the next edge in the clockwise order around it. A graph usually has\nseveral planar embeddings with different faces (only 3-connected planar graphs have\njust one), and the one here need not match NetworkX's.\n\nEdge direction, self-loops and parallel edges are ignored.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [list[list[int]]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   One list of node IDs per face, in order around it (the last node is joined back to\n    the first). Walks repeat a node that the face's boundary passes more than once, as\n    around a bridge or a cut node: a path `A - B` has the single face `[A, B]`, walked\n    along both sides of its edge. Isolated nodes lie on no face, and each connected\n    component with edges contributes its own outer face.\n:::\n\n## Raises {.doc-raises}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   If the graph is not planar.\n:::\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~check_planarity`) \n\n:   The embedding the faces are traced from.\n\n## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}\n\nEvery half-edge (an edge taken in one direction) lies on exactly one face, so tracing\nthe face of each not yet visited half-edge, with nodes visited in ID order and their\nneighbors in clockwise order, finds every face once in `O(n + e)` time after the\nplanarity test.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nA square with one diagonal has three faces: the two triangles and the outer square:\n\n::: {#eed89aa2 .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\ng = ec.Graph.from_edges(\n    [(\"A\", \"B\"), (\"B\", \"C\"), (\"C\", \"D\"), (\"D\", \"A\"), (\"A\", \"C\")]\n)\n\n[[g.get_label_from_id(n) for n in face] for face in g.planar_faces()]\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n[['A', 'B', 'C'], ['A', 'C', 'D'], ['A', 'D', 'C', 'B']]\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### Loops in a floor plan\n\nThe rooms of a two-story office are joined where a door leads from one to the other.\nDrawn flat, the plan has 5 faces, as Euler's formula requires for 20 rooms and 23 doors\n(`20 - 23 + 5 = 2`). Each face is a walk around its edge. A dead end, like the kitchen, is\nwalked in and back out, so it appears twice:\n\n::: {#548faaba .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\noffices = ec.offices()\nfaces = [\n    [offices.get_label_from_id(n) for n in face]\n    for face in offices.planar_faces()\n]\n\nlen(faces), [len(face) for face in faces]\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```\n(5, [9, 3, 23, 8, 3])\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nThe two smallest faces are triangles: the lobby, reception and West Hall 1 downstairs, and\ntwo offices joined to each other and to East Hall 2 upstairs:\n\n::: {#dabaf57a .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\n[face for face in faces if len(face) == 3]\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```\n[['Lobby', 'West Hall 1', 'Reception'],\n ['East Hall 2', 'Office 203', 'Office 204']]\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nFour of the faces are the loops of rooms you can walk around, and the fifth is everything\noutside. The doors on the loops, the ones that aren't bridges, are highlighted:\n\n::: {#59fbbba4 .cell execution_count=4}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nbridges = set(offices.bridges())\nloops = [\n    (offices.get_label_from_id(s), offices.get_label_from_id(t))\n    for edge, s, t in offices.get_edges_as_tuples()\n    if edge not in bridges\n]\n\noffices.plot(\n    layout=\"pos\",\n    highlight_edges=loops,\n    label_by=\"label\",\n    width=700,\n    height=520,\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=4}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-63cc1385:#d4d4d8;--ect-879b7ebc:#949497;--ect-b9f8de6d:#872d08;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark){.ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-63cc1385:#3f3f46;--ect-879b7ebc:#2c2c31;--ect-b9f8de6d:#ae500f;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}}:is(html.quarto-light, body.quarto-light, html[data-bs-theme=\"light\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"true\"], body.vscode-light, body.vscode-high-contrast-light, html[theme=\"light\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-63cc1385:#d4d4d8;--ect-879b7ebc:#949497;--ect-b9f8de6d:#872d08;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}:is(html.quarto-dark, body.quarto-dark, html[data-bs-theme=\"dark\"], [d